Just imagine:

A client sends:
GET/ api/ products

Well, the controller might receive this request. But, it doesnt know how to retrieve the products from the SqlServer.
Thats the job of the repositories.

Therefore, we want :

Client --Get Request--> ProductsController --Request Data --> ProductRepositories --Execute SQL Query using Dapper --> SqlServer.

Therefore, our Controller must use the repositories.

so, we do using POS.Api.Repositories;


After understanding why we need it :

Explaination on 
private readonly ProductRepository _productRepository;

private: private
readonly : Field can be assigned when declared or inside an constructor, but it cannot be reassigned later.
ProductRepository _productRepository: _productRepository is the field that holds the reference to the object of ProductRepository
and  can be used by the Controllers. 

So, we are not creating a object.




HOW ASP.NET works inside it :

Step A: We register the repository in Program.cs

builder.Services.AddScoped<ProductRepository>();

This tells ASP.NET Core:

“When a component needs a ProductRepository, provide an instance according to its scoped lifetime.”

At this point, we’re registering the type; we’re not necessarily creating an object immediately.

Step B: A request arrives for our controller
Suppose someone requests:
GET /api/products
ASP.NET Core determines that it needs to create a ProductsController to handle that request.

Step C: ASP.NET Core examines the constructor
public ProductsController(ProductRepository productRepository)
{
    _productRepository = productRepository;
}
ASP.NET Core sees that the constructor requires a ProductRepository object.

It checks its DI registrations and creates or obtains an appropriate instance.

Step D: ASP.NET Core supplies the object

Conceptually, the process looks like this:
Program.cs
    |
    | AddScoped<ProductRepository>()
    v
DI container knows how to provide the repository
    |
    | HTTP request arrives
    v
ASP.NET Core needs ProductsController
    |
    | Examines its constructor
    v
ProductsController(ProductRepository productRepository)
    |
    | DI supplies the repository object
    v
productRepository receives the reference
    |
    | Assignment
    v
_productRepository = productRepository;